[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/rus/BTR_152]
BTR-152 Armored Personnel Carrier M1950

During the World War II, the Soviet Army, unlike the German, British and US forces, did not have own APCs. This was due in part to a lack of suitable chassis, and because the industry was focused on making cars and armored vehicles. Lend-lease supplies from the US and Great Britain eased the gap, but these supplies ceased when the war ended. Developing a domestic APC became a primary task.
Such vehicles were already in the planning phases from wartime, but engineers had never settled upon a satisfactory design. Specifications for light and medium APCs, based on US vehicles, were finally laid down in 1945. Choosing chassis type – half-track or wheeled – remained one major issue. Having analyzed all the proposed variations, the all-wheel drive chassis of the ZIS-151 truck was chosen as the cheapest and readiest. The development of a medium APC, designated the ZIS-152, was finally launched in November of 1946.
The first prototypes had been completed by 1947, but tests and improvements took longer than planned. The new APC, re-designated the BTR-152, was finally issued on March 24, 1950, and entered mass production in July of the same year. Production ended in 1955, after a total of 4923 vehicles had been produced. Eventually the BTR-152 was replaced with a modified variant: the BTR-152V.
In terms of design, the BTR-152 was a 6x6 all-wheel drive conventional open-top APC. In addition to its 2 crew members, the spacious hull could carry up to 17 soldiers. The APC had optimally sloped bulletproof armor, and came armed with an SG-43 medium machine gun.
The BTR-152 and its variants were the primary Soviet Army APCs through the mid-1960s. They were frequently exported, and a number of these vehicles are still in active service in some countries. The BTR-152 has seen its share of combat – in Arab-Israeli conflicts in particular.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/rus/BA_10M_Kursk1943]
BA-10M Medium Armoured Car

The BA-10M medium armoured car was the top vehicle in its class in the USSR. In the autumn of 1936, the modernization of the BA-6 armoured car began at the Izhora plants. The upgrades of the new model, initially called the BA-6m armoured car, were the installation of a more powerful GAZ M-1 engine, and the mounting of a conical turret, as well as other minor modifications. Test operations were carried out from January 11 to March 17, 1937, and the new model was deemed an overall success. In April of 1937, serial production of the BA-6M began, after the  problems revealed during the tests were resolved. The upgraded version of the BA-6c was designated the BA-10.

Serial production of the armoured car was schedule to begin in 1937, but in fact the first vehicles were accepted for service only in 1938. The reason for the delay was the introduction of multiple minor design changes, which resulted in yet another renaming: BA-10A. The BA-10A saw its first combat use during the battle of Khalkhin Gol; its operational capabilities were rated as "fairly high". However, a serious shortcoming in its design was revealed - should the  fuel tank be damaged, the burning fuel  would flew down onto the crew, for it was situated right over their heads. This led to many casualties. By the end of 1939, the design of the armoured car was significantly improved -- the gas tanks were now mounted in armour housing on the outside of the car, on the rear wings. The redesign had some other minor improvements as well. The serial production of this modification, which was named BA-10M, began in December, 1939.

In design, the vehicle was an armoured hull mounted on a GAZ-AAA truck chassis. The armour of the vehicle provided protection from light munitions. The weaponry, which consisted of a 45mm gun and two machine guns, enabled it to engage a wide range of targets. Altogether, the armoured car was a success for its design purposes -- reconnaissance, guarding, and escort -- and it proved capable of engaging enemy armoured vehicles in ambushes.

Nevertheless, armoured cars of this type suffered heavy losses in the battles of the summer 1941, although several remained in service thereafter. Thus, on April 14, 1945 there were eleven BA-10 armoured cars under the command of 2nd Belorussian Front. Several vehicles of this model took part in battles against the Japanese Kwantung Army. And indeed, a few BA-10 vehicles were committed to the Battle of Kursk. Thus, on July 6, 1943 there were five BA-10 vehicles under the command of 1st Guard Tank Brigade.

November, 1941 marked the end of BA-10 production. Over all, a total of 3,331 armoured cars of this model had been produced.


[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/rus/BA_64]
BA-64 Light Armoured Car

In September of 1941. The GAZ design bureau began work on a new light armoured vehicle, a substitute for the BA-20 (the production of which was being gradually scaled down). On the other hand, the new vehicle was intended to replace the LB-62 armoured car, the serial production of which had been seriously hindered. It was decided to use GAZ-64 chassis for this new vehicle, which limited the size and weapons capability of the vehicle. The lessons learned from the study of the German SdKfz 221 armoured car were also taken into account in its design. The initial run of the vehicle had already taken place by January 9, 1942. That February it was tested, and these field tests revealed several shortcomings which led to some modernization - the wheel base was widened, the suspension improved, engine power was increased, and so on. The vehicle was accepted for service on March 14, and the first production samples were formally commissioned by the military in May of 1942. Starting on May 25, the GAZ was to begin production of new modified armoured cars, named BA-64B, but because the construction plant had been heavily damaged by bombings, only four-hundred and five BA-64B cars and one-thousand nineteen BA-64 cars were produced in 1943.

The BA-64 was a classical example of a practical, economical vehicle; it was simple and cheap. Unlike earlier Soviet armoured cars, the BA-64 enjoyed 4-wheel drive, which undoubtedly enhanced its passing and manoeuvring abilities. The armour of the vehicle protected it from bullets only; its weaponry consisted of a single DP machine gun, capable of engaging air targets. In the field, the armoured car was used primarily for reconnaissance, communications and escort, and was an integral part of tank and motor-rifle convoys. The vehicle was regularly used in combat, from the summer of 1942 through the end of the war. A good many of these vehicles took part in the Battle of Kursk. For example, there were fifty four BA-64 in 29th Tank Corps of 5th Guards Tank Army, and there were eighteen BA-64 cars, six BA-20 cars, five BA-10 cars, two FAI-M cars, and five Universal Carriers in the ranks of 3rd Mechanized Corps of 1st Tank Army as of June 4, 1943.

Before its closing in February of 1946, the GAZ plant had produced a total of 3,903 BA-64 cars and 5,160 BA-64B cars.


[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/rus/Universal_Carrier_Mk_I_Kursk1943]
Universal Carrier Mk.I Tracked Military Vehicle

The Universal Carrier vehicle, commonly known as the Bren Gun Carrier, was designed by Vickers-Armstrong based on the Carrier Mk I artillery tug and the Vickers-Carden-Loyd tankette. The Universal Carrier, adopted for service in 1938, was intended for transporting support weapons, reconnaissance, and other utility tasks. After Great Britain entered the war, production was greatly expanded, encompassing multiple factories in Canada, the US, Australia and New Zealand, which produced most of the vehicles. Several variants were produced, adopted to unique conditions at each factory, and differing in the number of road wheels, engine types, and other changes. Several additional variants were produced, including self-propelled flamethrowers, mortar carriers, and self-propelled guns.

In design, the vehicle was a lightly armoured open-top hull on a tracked chassis. The vehicle was armed with a single Bren machine gun, or a Boys anti-tank rifle, located in the frontal armour of the AFV. The vehicle could also be equipped with an AAA mount; the Soviets sometimes equipped their lend-lease variants with a 12.7mm DShK machine gun. In addition to the crew of two, the vehicle could carry 3 to 4 passengers. The vehicle’s extremely low height made it easy to camouflage. The vehicle could reach speeds of up to 50 km/h. Altogether, while having some disadvantages such as low carrying capacity and poor protection for passengers, the vehicle proved rather successful and was produced in large numbers.

The vehicle was used throughout the war by the United Kingdom and other Allied countries. The British used their Bren Carriers with infantry and tank regiments throughout the world, in Europe, North Africa, and the Pacific. In addition to other Commonwealth nations, Bren carriers were also used by the Chinese, and the Soviets received 2,008 vehicles via the lend-lease program, mostly produced in Canada. The Soviets generally used them in the same role as the British, with infantry and tank regiment recon units. Used in 1942-1945, the vehicles proved popular with the Russians, participating in such pivotal battles as Kursk, where just a single mechanized corps of the 1st Tank Army had 5 UCs on hand on June 4th, 1943.

By 1945, a total of 89,595 Universal Carriers were built by multiple manufacturers, easily making it the most mass-produced light armoured fighting vehicle of WWII.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/rus/nkl_26]
Climatic peculiarities of the northern areas of Soviet Union, where snow stays on the ground for a long time, paved the way for a broad application of snowmobiles for transporting mail, passengers and cargo. In the pre-war period, designer M. Andreyev developed the OSGA-4 and OSGA-6 snowmobiles for these purposes. Positive experience in using snowmobiles in the Finnish campaign of 1939-1940 resulted in the creation of a snowmobile capable of conducting reconnaissance and, if necessary, taking part in combat operations. The snowmobile was developed by Andreyev and entered the Red Army service under the designation of “NKL-26 Combat Snowmobile”. It had a wooden frameless body, and the front was protected by an anti-bullet armor plate. The body front contained the control section, where the driver was situated. The eye slit with a glass block from the BA-20 armored car was built into the front panel to enable the driver to look  at the road in front. The combat section was located behind the control section. It housed the 7.62-mm DT tank machine-gun on a ring mount, protected by a light gun shield. Machine-gun fire was conducted by the snowmobile commander. The horizontal firing angle amounted to 300 degrees, while horizontal angles varied from –14 to 40 degrees. The machine-gun’s ammunition loadout was as high as 1,000 cartridges. The back of the snowmobile housed the radial five-cylinder air-cooled carburetor engine developing 100 hp, which enabled the snowmobile to reach a speed of up to 75 km/h. 
The fuel tank located next to the engine held 100 liters of petroleum, which ensured a range of 100 km when riding on a snow-covered road and up to 175 km when riding on a rural road with a thick snowpath. The snowmobile rode on four wooden skis attached to the hull with the help of an independent suspension using spiral springs. Cable steering controlled both front and rear skis. The NKL-26 was in the inventory of specialized snowmobile battalions. In addition, the battalions used the NKL-16 transport and landing snowmobiles without armor or armament. The loading section of that snowmobile could fit  four armed soldiers or cargo. 

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/usa/m6_staghound]
The competition among projects to design a medium three-axle armored car, held by the U.S. Army in 1941, was won by the T17 of the Ford Co. The vehicle was not very successful, but 250 vehicles were manufactured, and some of them were battle-tested in North Africa. The first sample of the T17E1 vehicle assembled by Chevrolet in September 1941 also took part in the competition. The T17E1 was a two-axle vehicle, a bit lighter and shorter than the T17, but had the same turret. In June 1942, the T17E1 was standardized and designated M6 Medium Armored Car, but the U.S. army did not need such a vehicle. The T17E1 attracted much more interest in Great Britain. The order for 2,000 vehicles of this type was placed in January 1942. In the British army, the vehicle was designated Staghound. Nearly 4,000 vehicles of the Staghound family were manufactured.
The T17E1 can easily be classified as a wheeled tank. Its specifications are comparable to that of the M3A1 Light Tank. Moreover, it was the heaviest armored car of World War II. An impressive fuel capacity indicates clearly that the vehicle was designed for combat operations in North Africa, but the Staghound was completed too late for that. At the end of the war, the 37-mm M6 gun no longer had enough firepower to combat enemy tanks. This problem was solved in a unique way, when the regular turret was replaced with the turret of a Cruiser Tank Mk.VI Crusader III, and the 6 pdr gun was replaced with the 75-mm ROQF Mk. V gun. Such vehicles were designated Staghound III.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/usa/M8_Greyhound]
M8 Light Armored Car

In July of 1941, US military command decided that a new tank destroyer was to be developed to replace the M6 GMC, a standard 37mm gun mounted on a regular 3/4 ton truck. The new vehicle was to be an armored 6x6, armed with the same 37mm gun. Prototypes were made by Studebaker (designated the T21), Ford (designated the T22) and Chrysler (designated the T23). The Ford vehicle was selected in April of 1942. The car was designated the M8, but as it nevertheless required several improvements, it only entered mass production in March of 1943. By that time, it had become clear that the 37mm gun could not inflict sufficient damage on German armored vehicles, so the role of the M8 had changed to reconnaissance.
In terms of design, the M8 was a medium 6x6 all-wheel drive armored car (though it was officially categorized as a light vehicle). It carried a 37mm gun and a coaxial machine gun, as well as a large-caliber anti-aircraft machine gun in an open-top turret. It boasted bulletproof armor, but while its frontal plate could protect from large-caliber machine gun fire, its side and rear plates could only withstand regular machine gun fire. In general, the vehicle was quite good in fulfilling its role, proving itself quite capable of dealing with infantry and lightly armored vehicles.
From mid-1943, the M8 actively fought both for the US and their British allies, who had designated the lend-leased car the “Greyhound”. Mass production continued until June 1945, over which time a total of 8523 armored cars were manufactured.
The M8 also saw extensive use in the Korean War. Supplied to the South Korea by the US, these armored cars were the country’s only armored vehicles at the beginning of the war.
Numerous M8 cars were supplied to the allies of the US during the postwar years. In some parts of the world, these vehicles are still in service.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/usa/M3A1_Halftrack]
Carrier, Personnel Half-track M3A1

Half-track APCs had been in development at several US companies since the early 1930s, but by the end of the decade no acceptable prototypes had yet been produced. Naturally, development was spurred on by the war, and already in 1940 Diamond Motor Company created the T8 and T14 prototypes, based on their M3 Scout Car. In October of 1940, two new armored cars were adopted, named the M3 and M2. The M3 had a 25cm longer body, but the main difference was one of purpose: the M2 was initially designed as an artillery tractor, and therefore had fewer seats to accommodate more ammunition. Both vehicles entered mass production in the spring of 1941.
In 1943, based on the combat data collected in Africa, it was decided to augment the half-track’s armaments with a forward 12.7mm large-caliber machine gun mounted above the commander’s seat. The machine gun was surrounded bulletproof armor. Designated the M3А1, this new vehicle entered mass production late in 1943, and numerous older M3 cars were upgraded to M3А1.
In terms of design, the M3А1 was an open-top front-wheel drive half-track with 12 personnel seats plus a driver’s seat. The car was armed with a 12.7mm Browning M2HB machine gun and a 7.62mm Browning M1919A4 machine gun, usually mounted in the rear. In addition, another or two liquid-cooled 7.62mm machine guns were mounted on the sides. In most respects, the M3 surpassed the German SdKfz 251. Its advantages included front-wheel drive for improved mobility, more powerful armaments, and a hull spacious enough for a full infantry squad. The car’s main drawback was weaker armor, which could be penetrated by an armor-piercing rifle bullet fired at close distance.
M3A1 half-tracks fought much in Italy, France, and the Pacific islands. During the postwar years, these half-tracks were exported and fought in a number of armed conflicts, including the Korean War.
A number of specialized vehicles were based on M3, including self-propelled artillery, anti-aircraft guns, mortars, and so on.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/usa/M3A1_Halftrack_afrika]
Carrier, Personnel Half-track M3 armored vehicle

Several companies competed to produce a new half-track for the US Army starting in 1932. The work proceeded slowly, with no decision being made as late as 1939. The deteriorating situation in Europe and the Pacific however led to a quickened pace, and by 1940 White Motor Company created two prototypes designated T8 and T14, both based on their M3 Scout Car. 

In October of 1940 both prototypes went in production, one as the M3 and the other as the M2 Half-Track. The two half-tracks different in length, M3 being 10 inches longer, as well as their intended duties. The M2 was designed as a prime mover, with fewer passenger seats in the rear, instead carrying additional ammo boxes. Both half-tracks were accepted for service in the spring of 1941.

Technically, the M3 was an open half-tracked armored car with a wheeled front axle. The vehicle could carry a driver and 12 fully equipped passengers. It was armed with a single .30-cal M1919 Browning machine gun, located in a ring mount in the front of the vehicle. The M3 was a very successful vehicle, generally comparable to the German Sd Kfz 251. The advantages of the M3 included a wheeled front axle, which made it very capable off-road, as well as a spacious fighting compartment, which could accommodate a fully equipped infantry squad. The main disadvantage was rather weak armor, easily penetrated by an armor-piercing rifle bullet. 

M3 half-tracks were widely used on all fronts, including fighting in North Africa, Italy, France, and the Pacific. For example, during Battle of the Kasserine pass, the 6th Battalion of the 1st Armored Regiment, which had 230 M3s on hand, was practically wiped out, with most half-tracks ending up being captured by the Germans, which then saw service with the Axis. The M3s were also sent to other Allies via lend-lease, including the UK and the USSR. A total of 12,499 M3s and a further 4,625 M5 Half-Tracks (a licensed M3 manufactured by the International Harvester Company) were produced in 1942 and 1943, after which the M3 was supplanted by an improved M3A1 variant.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/fr/panhard_178_(25mm_gun)]
Starting from the early 1930s, the French army was developing a concept of combat vehicles for mechanized cavalry units. According to that concept, mechanized cavalry units were to receive Automitrailleuse de Decouverte (AMD) medium armored cars, Automitrailleuse de Reconnaissance (AMR) light reconnaissance tanks, as well as Automitrailleuse de Combat (AMC) close support tanks. The AMD program requirements for an armored car were as follows: armor 5-8 mm thick, average speed of 40 km/h, maximum speed of 70 km/h, and armament consisting of a 20-mm quick-firing gun. 
The competition among AMD vehicle projects involved Renault, Berliet and Latil companies. The last to join was the Panhard company, which created the first prototype of its armored vehicle in October 1933. The prototype had a Vincennes turret, identical to those of the AMR 35ZT reconnaissance tank, and its armament consisted of the 13.2-mm Hotchkiss machine-gun. Tests showed that the vehicle was too heavy and did not fit the requirement specifications. The prototype was seriously upgraded in February 1934, and this time Panhard managed to surpass the specified requirements. The vehicle entered inventory under the designation of AMD 35 in the autumn that year. Mass-produced vehicles received the new APX-3 turret, and the 13.2-mm Hotchkiss AMD 35 machine-gun was replaced with the 25-mm Hotchkiss SA-34 automatic gun and the 7.5-mm Reibel Mle.31 machine-gun. Armor protection of the AMD 35 surpassed requirements, too, reaching 20 mm in thickness at the front hull and 26 mm at the turret front. As many as 526 vehicles of this type, as well as 24 command vehicles, where the APX-3 turret was replaced with an immovable cockpit, and the ER27 heavy radio added, were manufactured from 1934 to 1940. Moreover, a prototype fitted with a new welded Renault turret and a 47-mm SA-35 gun was made. Tthe baseline model was produced in two series, different from each other in minor details. Due to an acute shortage of APX-3 turrets, a small number of armored cars with an immovable cockpit identical to that of the AMD 35 command model and armed with one 7.5-mm machine gun were made in June 1940.
At the time of development, the AMD 35 was one of the best medium armored cars in the world. Its firepower was only inferior to that of the Soviet BA-3/BA-6/BA-10 armored cars, but its cross-country capability was better thanks to the employment of an all-wheel-drive chassis. The AMD 35 was among the few types of trophy hardware used by German troops without serious redesign (except for installation of new radios). After the war, the upgraded model of the vehicle, designated Panhard 178B, was used by French forces until the mid-1950s.
 
[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/fr/panhard_amc_p_16]
Adolf Kegresse made his first attempt at designing an armored halftrack vehicle in 1915. Under the supervision of the talented French designer, who also headed the Russian Emperor’s Garage, a project was developed to install a tracked propulsive device instead of the rear axle of the Austin armored car, manufactured on a license at the Putilovsky Plant (Petersburg). The vehicle, designated Austin-Kegresse, was put into mass production in 1918, and by that time Adolf Kegresse had emigrated to France. In his home country, Kegresse got the Citroen company interested in his developments. The Citroen-Kegresse M23 halftrack armored car, with the concept that had a lot in common with that of the Austin-Kegresse, was designed in 1923. However, the capacity of the Citroen company turned out to be quite limited, and it failed to comply even with orders for common halftrack vehicles, let alone armored cars. As a result, halftrack armored cars designed by Kegresse jointly with the Schneider company were produced in high volumes.
The Schneider-Kegresse AMC P16 Mle.1929 became the most successful armored car. The automobile was a derivative of the Schneider-Kegresse P16 Mle.28 project, differing from the latter in body and turret design. Its armament consisted of the 37-mm Puteaux SA-18 gun and coaxial 7.5-mm Chatelerault Mle.24/29 machine-gun. 
The Schneider-Kegresse AMC P16 Mle.1929 was distinguished for advanced cross-country capability and quite powerful armament. However, the vehicle had become very much outdated by 1940, and the rubber block tracks designed by Kegresse were not distinguished for their reliability. 

[EncDescription_data/units/ArmoredCar/Ita/Autoblinda_40]
Autoblinda AB40 (armored car AB40)
In 1937 the companies SPA and Analso started the creation of a new armored motorcar, destined to replace the Lancia I.Z. vehicles of the WWI period. In 1939 the prototype model of the motorcar was tested, and in the end of the same year it had been launched in a serial production with the index Autoblinda AB40.

Constructively the machine constituted a four-wheel machinegun motorcar with a bulletproof armor. Every car wheel had an independent spring suspension and was controllable. The motorcar was equipped with a rear control platform, which allowed to quickly leave a combat in reverse gear without wasting time on turnback.

Altogether, the machine proved itself well, although its weaponry and armor had already been considered weak for the beginning of the 1940s.

Autoblinda AB40 was large-scalely produced during 1940-1941, whereupon its modification AB41 was launched. In total, 139 machines had been produced, which were used in North Africa and in the Balkans.

[EncDescription_data/units/ArmoredCar/Ita/Autoblinda_41]
Autoblinda AB41 (armored car AB41)

The very first battles Autoblinda AB40 showed that the weaponry of the motorcar needed reinforcing, which resulted into creating the AB41 modification in 1941. The new machine resembled the predecessor mainly in the weaponry - coaxial machineguns in the turret were replaced by a 20mm automatic gun and a coaxial machinegun. Having a good ballistics, the gun allowed the machine to successfully battle with the light armor of the enemy - armored vehicles, armored personnel carriers, some light tanks as well as effectively suppressing MG nests.

Autoblinda AB41 proved themselves very well in the military, despite a quite weak armoring (which protected only from bullets) and non-pressurized body assembled with bolts and rivets, which did not shut out hitting with leaded drippings through the slits. These machines were especially successful in the North Africa in reconnaissance and attacking enemy transport convoys.

AB41 was actively used in the North Africa, in the Balkans, in Sicily, and continental Italy. After the capitulation of Italy, a number of armored motorcars got to the Germans, who were actively using AB41 with the index Panzerspähwagen AB41 201(i) and even continued its serial manufacturing.

In total, 508 armor motorcars AB41 were manufactured during 1941-43, which made the largest amount in the Italian army, aside of that, 23 more machines were manufactured under German control. In course of time the manufacturing of AB41 was replaced with a new, even more advanced modification AB43.

Except Italian and German armies, a small amount of AB41s was used by Romanian and Hungarian forces as well as by Yugoslavian partisans.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/SdKfz_251_9_Ausf_C_Kursk1943]
SdKfz 251/9 Ausf.C (gun support vehicle based on the SdKfz 251C medium half-track armoured personnel carrier)

The SdKfz 251/9 gun support vehicle was a specialized vehicle based on the design of the SdKfz 251 medium armoured personnel carrier. The need for vehicles of this type emerged after the roles of the Pz.IV medium tanks and StuG III SPGs evolved from the role of direct infantry support to antitank actions. Many of their 7.5сm KwK L/24 short-barrel guns had been removed, and as the result of this, on March 31, 1949, Büssing NAG was assigned the task of installing a 75mm short-barrel gun on the SdKfz 251 armoured personnel carrier. In June of 1942, two prototypes were dispatched to the Eastern Front for field trials. The army approved of the new vehicle, and the first 150 units were delivered within the same month.

The original design of the personnel carrier had been changed drastically: the commander's station was removed, and a special stand with a 7.5cm KwK L/24 gun and its shield was mounted in the fighting cab. This required cutting off the top part of the chassis, and equipping the vehicle with additional compartments for holding ammunition, equipment, and radio sets.

The vehicle proved quite a success, and was produced through the war's end. In fact, the carrier had several modifications. The SdKfz 251 Ausf.D chassis was used from September 1943 onwards; later, vehicles with an alternative stand fixture were produced, with a larger bulkhead on the front, and a rear machine gun installed. The SdKfz 251/9 was in the service of heavy companies of mechanized battalions of panzergrenadier regiments, which were under the command of tank and panzergrenadier divisions of the Wehrmacht and the SS. On the battlefield the vehicles were often used against infantry, machine-gun emplacements, mortars, and field artillery positions. However, engagement against enemy tanks was considered undesirable. Vehicles of this type were widely used through the end of the war.


[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/SdKfz_251_10_Ausf_C]
SdKfz 251/10 Ausf.C (platoon commander's vehicle, based on the SdKfz 251C medium half-track armoured personnel carrier)

The platoon commander's SdKfz 251/10 was another vehicle based on the SdKfz 251 personnel carrier. It was equipped with a 37mm Pak 35/36 antitank gun, along with other minor changes in the design to accommodate an ammunition unit mounted in the fighting compartment.

This vehicle often housed the commanders of an armoured panzergrenadier platoon, and was also used as a command vehicle for APC scout platoons. The gun installed on the vehicle could successfully engage most enemy tanks until 1941, but its piercing effect was clearly insufficient against T-34 tanks. Nevertheless, the production of SdKfz 251/10 continued, though its guns were rarely aimed at tanks, but rather at machine gun emplacements and positions of battalion and regiment artillery. Essentially, the APC evolved into a light armoured car with direct infantry support. However, the unit's ammunition was augmented by composite shells and super-calibre hollow charge grenades, which enabled the vehicle to stand against T-34 and KV tanks, in close range.

SdKfz 251/10 was produced in several versions, depending on the base chassis. In addition, the vehicles differed from each other by their dimensions and the size of their apron shield; the shields of some APCs (especially the late series) were much smaller or were entirely absent. These SdKfz 251/10 vehicles were widely used until the end of the war, on every front line.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/SdKfz_251_1_Ausf_C]
Medium Half-Track Armoured Car Mittlerer Schützenpanzerwagen Sd Kfz 251/1 Ausf.C

The Sd Kfz 251 armoured fighting vehicle was a further modification of a 3-ton Sd Kfz 11 half-track prime mover. First prototypes were produced by Hanomag in 1937. After a series of improvements, the half-track was accepted for service, and first production vehicles rolled off the assembly line in 1939. The initial Ausf.A and Ausf.B variants had a brief production run, numbering comparatively few examples. The first truly mass-produced variant was the Ausf.C, introduced in late 1940. The new modification addressed some issues discovered in the field, and also made the vehicle easier to manufacture. The changes included a straight frontal armour plate, armoured air intake covers (which easily distinguish this modification from others), and spare part boxes moved to the rear.

The Sd Kfz 251 was a half-track vehicle with an open-top armoured hull. The vehicle was spacious enough to transport a full infantry squad with all their equipment. The vehicle’s armour was designed to withstand small arms fire. The vehicle was armed with a machine gun behind an armour shield in the front section of the vehicle, which could be fired by the AFV commander when needed. In addition, the rear of the vehicle contained a turret which could be equipped with one of the infantry squad machine guns; the turret could then be used in an anti-aircraft role.

Sd Kfz 251 Ausf.C was involved in combat since 1941 on all fronts, usually used by panzer and panzergrenadier divisions. Altogether the vehicle proved very successful, and is considered one of the best in its class. Over 20 different variants of the fighting vehicle were produced, including mortar carriers, light anti-tank gun mounts, communications vehicles, and others. The Ausf.C variant remained in production until September of 1943, when it was replaced by a the technologically advanced Ausf.D variant.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/SdKfz_250_1_alt]
Light Armoured Half-Track Leichter Schützenpanzerwagen Sd Kfz 250 “Alt"

In the late 1930s Germany began development of a new light armoured vehicle to compliment the recently adopted Sd Kfz 251. The new prototype was based on the Sd Kfz 10 half-track prime mover. The chassis of the vehicle was produced by Demag, and the armoured hull by Bussing-NAG. The vehicle’s design phase lasted for some time, with first prototypes appearing only in 1939, and serial production beginning in June of 1941.

The Sd Kfz 250 was a light half-track with an open-top armoured hull. The vehicle was spacious enough to transport a full infantry squad with all equipment. The vehicle’s armour was designed to withstand small arms fire. The vehicle was armed with a machine-gun behind an armour shield in the front section of the vehicle, which could be fired by the AFV commander when needed. In addition, the rear of the vehicle contained a turret which could be equipped with one of the infantry squad machine guns; the turret could then be used in an anti-aircraft role.

Sd Kfz 250 “Alt" was involved in combat since 1941 on all fronts, usually used by reconnaissance battalions of panzer and panzergrenadier divisions. The AFV proved reliable, although its small carrying capacity was often a disadvantage. Over 20 different variants of the fighting vehicle were produced, including mortar carriers, light anti-tank gun mounts, artillery support vehicles, self-propelled AA, communications vehicles, ammunition carriers, and others. The “Alte" (old) variant remained in production until October of 1943, when it was supplanted by a technologically superior Sd Kfz 250 “Neu".

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/SdKfz_231_early]
Schwerer Panzerspähwagen 8-Rad (early)

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/SdKfz_231]
Schwerer Panzerspähwagen 8-Rad

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/SdKfz_232_8Rad]
Heavy Armoured Communications Vehicle, 8-wheel Schwere Panzerspähwagen (FU) 8-rad Sd Kfz 232
  
By 1935 Germany‘s rearmament was in full swing. One of the many new projects was a replacement for the Sd Kfz 231 six-wheel armoured car, which was found to be somewhat lacking in performance. The new prototype, for the first time anywhere in the world, used an eight-wheel drive system. Prototype trials were finished in 1936, and the new armoured car quickly entered production.

Initially the vehicle received the same designation as the six-wheel Sd Kfz 31, with the suffix 8-rad, or 8-wheel. Another variant of the prototype also entered production in 1936 as Sd Kfz 232 heavy communications vehicle, carrying a powerful medium range radio and a portable antenna.
The Sd Kfz 232 was an all-wheel drive vehicle, with all eight wheels directly receiving torque from the engine. Its armour was designed to withstand small arms fire, and the armour was sloped, increasing its effectiveness. With the driver’s and commander’s seats attached to the turret, the vehicle could easily travel forward as well as in reverse. The armoured car was armed with a 20mm cannon and a coaxial machine gun, which allowed it to engage other light armoured vehicles. A distinguishing characteristic of the Sd Kfz 232 was its powerful FuG 12 radio with a large bedstead antenna, which made the vehicle very visible on the battlefield. During the subsequent years the vehicle went through several modifications. In May 1942, the frontal armour was increased to 30mm, protecting the vehicle from large-calibre machine guns and anti-tank rifles. Existing examples were retrofitted with a so-called Pakschutz device, a pocket of sorts made up of 8mm armour plates, installed on the front of the vehicle 60 cm ahead of the frontal armour plate. Such novel armour arrangement made the vehicle more survivable when facing heavy machine guns and anti-tank rifles.

The armoured vehicle was heavily used throughout the war in all theatres, being standard equipment of panzer and panzergrenadier division reconnaissance battalions. For example, in July 1943 Panzergrenadier-Division Grossdeutschland had 6 Sd Kfz 232 8-pads in its Aufklärungs-Abteilung (reconnaissance battalion). The vehicles were mainly used to maintain communications between the recon battalion and division headquarters. In North Africa, due to the fluidity of combat, vehicles like the Sd Kfz 232 played an important part. This very capable vehicle remained in production until September of 1943. A total of 607 Sd Kfz 231s and Sd Kfz 232s were produced.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/SdKfz_222]
Leichter Panzerspähwagen (2 cm) Sd Kfz 222 Light Armoured Reconnaissance Vehicle

Germany began developing a replacement for the clearly obsolescent Sd Kfz 13 light armoured car in 1934. By 1936 several prototypes were built by a number of companies, and a Horch 801 prototype made by Eisenwerk Weserhütte of Bad Oeynhausen was soon accepted for production, initially designated Sd Kfz 221 and armed with a single MG34 machine gun. By 1936, an updated version entered production, designated Sd Kfz 222 and armed with a 20mm autocannon.
The Sd Kfz 222 was a light 4x4 vehicle with good armour. The sides of the car were sloped, greatly increasing its ability to withstand small arms fire. The vehicle was armed with a 20mm cannon and a 7.92mm machine gun, allowing it to engage other light armoured vehicles. Redesigned turrets on later models allowed the weapons to be elevated almost vertically, giving the vehicle the ability to engage aerial targets as well.

The vehicle went through a series of modifications. By May of 1942, a variant with an improved chassis and a more powerful engine was introduced. Frontal armour was increased to 30mm, protecting the vehicle from large-calibre machine guns and anti-tank rifles. The armoured car was heavily used throughout the war on all fronts, being standard equipment of panzer and panzergrenadier division reconnaissance battalions. For example, in July 1943 Panzergrenadier-Division Grossdeutschland had 22 Sd Kfz 222s in its Aufklärungs-Abteilung (reconnaissance battalion). The armoured cars were used for reconnaissance, communications, transport column escort, and anti-guerrilla duties. In North Africa, usually lacking a continuous front line, constant reconnaissance by armoured cars was especially crucial. In the early part of the war armoured cars were often at the forefront of fighting.

The vehicle remained in production until June of 1943. 989 units were produced by several manufacturers.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/sdkfz_251_1_ausf_d]
Production of the last model of the Sd.Kfz.251 APC – the Sd.Kfz.251 Ausf.D, began in the autumn of 1943. It no longer had the catwalk on the sides of the hood, which wase characteristic of the Sd.Kfz.251 Ausf.C, and the body shape changed. SPTA boxes, earlier mounted on the hull sides, were now located inside the hull. Moreover, onboard observation devices were reinforced with bulletproof glass. This version saw the highest production output, as out of 15,000 Sd.Kfz.251 vehicles manufactured, the Sd.Kfz.251 Ausf.D vehicles alone amounted to 10,500. A total of 23 varieties of the Sd.Kfz.251 Ausf.D were available, ranging from the APC to an anti-aircraft combat vehicle.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/sdkfz_251_9_ausf_d]
SdKfz.251/9 was an armored car fitted with 75mm short-barreled KwK-37 L/24 (designated K-51 later), which was used on Pz.Kpfw.IV Ausf.A - F-1 tanks earlier. These armored cars were issued to grenadier units.
Front armor shape changed somewhat in comparison with a regular SdKfz.251 and the gun was protected by a small rectangular shield. Gun carriage was mounted on a pedestal fastened to the floor of the fighting compartment. Gun could be elevated from -10 to +12 degrees and rotated up to 12 degrees to each side. Telescopic sight SfiZF-1 was installed, ammo load consisted of 52 AP and HE shells.
SdKfz.251/9 weighed 8.53 tons fully laden, required 5 men crew, and carried one 7.92mm MG-42 machinegun in addition to main gun.
The prototype was made by Bussing - NAG during March 1942, and two of them were sent to East front at July 1942. These trials were successful and 150 such vehicles were ordered at the same month. German soldiers called them 'Stummel' (cigarette end) because of short gun barrel.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/sdkfz_251_17_ausf_c]
SdKfz.251/17 was a self-propelled anti-aircraft gun (automatic 20mm Flak-38 AA). It weighed 8.8 tons and required 4 - 6 people crew. It's maximal height was 2.25 meters.
Total 211 units were made. First ones (using SdKfz.251 Ausf.A and В chassis) were tested at 26 October 1943. SdKfz.251 Ausf. C - based series were also produced. Near the end of the war production of new series started, equipped with 360-degrees gun mount.
Gun in late series vehicles was placed in a small turret. Sides were made foldable to maximize sector of fire.
These vehicles usually were issued to Luftwaffe (AA gun units), in addition, some tank units also had them.
 
[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/sdkfz_251_22_ausf_d]
Lack of AT artillery forced Germans to create one more SdKfz.251-based armored car - 7,5-cm Pak.40 L/46 Mittlerer Schutzenpanzerwagen, equipped with 75mm Pak-40/1 AT gun. The gun was mounted in the same way like it was done on SdKfz.234/4 "Puma". Gun carriage was placed immediately behind the driver seat, part of the roof was removed to make room for gun suspension mechanism. Horizontal aiming angles were -20 degrees to the left and 18 degrees to the right, elevation angles were limited from -3 to +48 degrees. Ammunition load consisted of 22 shots (17 shells in container to the right of the gun and 5 in container below the gun). Gun crew consisted of 4 crewmen. Commander was located in the rear, while gunner sat on the collapsible wooden seat to the left of the gun, near the two loaders.
The armored car was too overloaded, which often caused chassis and front wheels failures. SdKfz.251/22 prototype was ready at 3 December 1944, mass production started during the same month. Panzer divisions "model 1945" should have had 9 SdKfz.251/22 in artillery batteries, 3 in recon battalions and 6 in tank units. Total 268 were produced.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/ger/sdkfz_251_23_ausf_d]
SdKfz.251/23 was armed with 20mm KwK-38 automatic gun and 7.92mm MG-42 machine gun set up in a Hangelaffete-28 turret (SdKfz.234/1, SdKfz.222 and Aufklaerungpanzer 38(t) had the same turret). Gun elevation could be adjusted from – 10 to + 85 degrees. This vehicle required 4-men crew. FuG-12 radio equipment was installed.
This was a final variant in SdKfz.251 series, SdKfz.251/23 used Ausf.D chassis. Mass production of SdKfz.251/23 started at December 1944.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/uk/Daimler_Mk_I]
Daimler Armoured Car, Mk.I

United Kingdom created several armoured car designs in 1939-1941, especially after the fall of France, when the British Isles were under real threat of invasion. Many of these armoured vehicles were rather poorly suited for combat.

A new armoured car entered production in 1939, designated Daimler Scout Car. This light and rather unimpressive looking vehicle lacked a turret, and was armed with a single .303 Bren machine gun. Nevertheless, it proved rather successful, and remained in production until 1945. However, a more powerful armoured car based on it was also designed the same year.
Adding extra armour changed the car’s appearance, and the final design little resembled its parent. It went into production in July of 1941 The Daimler Mk.I was a medium all-wheel-drive armoured car. The hull was hexagonal. Sloped armour increased effectiveness, but was not designed to withstand anything beyond small arms fire. A distinguishing feature of the AFV was its comparatively powerful armament, a 2pdr (40mm) gun which could engage not only other light armoured vehicles, but also light tanks, and, under favourable conditions, even medium tanks. The latter capability was greatly expanded after the introduction of the so-called Littlejohn tapered bore adaptor, which allowed the gun to fire high velocity APCNR rounds. Altogether, the Daimler Armoured Car is deservingly considered the best British armoured fighting vehicle of the war, almost similar to light tanks in its capabilities.

Daimler Mk.I served in North Africa (where its off-road capabilities and long range proved very useful), in Italy, Greece, France, and South-East Asia. The vehicles were mainly used for reconnaissance and patrol duties, becoming very popular with the troops. The vehicle remained in production until the end of the war. A total of 2,277 Daimler Mk.I and Mk.IIs were produced, with the latter having only minor differences from Mk.I.


[EncDescription_data/Units/ArmoredCar/UK/Universal_Carrier_Mk_I]
Universal Carrier Mk.I Tracked Military Vehicle

The Universal Carrier vehicle, commonly known as the Bren Gun Carrier, was designed by Vickers-Armstrong based on the Carrier Mk I artillery tug and the Vickers-Carden-Loyd tankette. The Universal Carrier, adopted for service in 1938, was intended for transporting support weapons, reconnaissance, and other utility tasks. After Great Britain entered the war, production was greatly expanded, encompassing multiple factories in Canada, US, Australia and New Zealand, which produced most of the vehicles. Several variants were produced, adopted to unique conditions at each factory, and differing in the number of road wheels, engine types, and other changes. Several additional variants were produced, including self-propelled flamethrowers, mortar carriers, and self-propelled guns.

In design, the vehicle was a lightly armoured open-top hull on a tracked chassis. The vehicle was armed with a single Bren machine gun, or a Boys anti-tank rifle, located in the frontal armour of the AFV. The vehicle could also be equipped with an AAA mount; the Soviets sometimes equipped their lend-lease variants with a 12.7mm DShK machine gun. In addition to the crew of two, the vehicle could carry 3 to 4 passengers. The vehicle’s extremely low height made it easy to camouflage. The vehicle could reach speeds of up to 50 km/h. Altogether, while having some disadvantages such as low carrying capacity and poor protection for passengers, the vehicle proved rather successful and was produced in large numbers.

The vehicle was used throughout the war by United Kingdom and other Allied countries. The British used their Bren Carriers with infantry and tank regiments throughout the world, in Europe, North Africa, and the Pacific. In addition to other Commonwealth nations, Bren carriers were also used by the Chinese, and the Soviets received 2,008 examples via the lend-lease program, mostly produced in Canada. The Soviets generally used them in the same role as the British, with infantry and tank regiment recon units. Used in 1942-1945, the vehicles proved popular with the Russians, participating in such pivotal battles as Kursk, where just a single mechanized corps of the 1st Tank Army had 5 UCs on hand on June 4th, 1943.

By 1945, a total of 89,595 Universal Carriers were built by multiple manufacturers, easily making it the most mass-produced light armoured fighting vehicle of WWII.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/uk/Daimler_Littlejohn]
Daimler Armoured Car M1941 with Littlejohn adaptor

By 1943, it had become clear that the 2-pounder 40mm gun, which had seen wide use on tanks and armoured cars, did not offer sufficient penetration against the latest reinforced German armoured vehicles. Because of this, the British adopted more powerful 57mm and 75mm guns, though it remained impossible to mount such guns on armoured cars and light tanks.

Since the 1930s, the Czech engineer Janacek had been working on a conical gun nozzle which would significantly improve muzzle velocity and piercing performance. When Germans occupied Czechoslovakia, he immigrated to Great Britain and continued his work. Ultimately, his solution -- named the "Littlejohn adaptor" -- was adopted by the British army in 1943.

In terms of design, Littlejohn was a detachable conical adaptor for 2-pounder guns. When installed, the gun could only fire special subcalibre squash-band shells. Upon entering the tapered portion of the adaptor, the shell was compressed down to about 30mm. The longer bore, combined with increased pressure, improved the shell’s muzzle velocity and piercing penetration, allowing it to damage even well-armoured vehicles when fired at close range. The adaptor could be detached, if needed, so the gun could fire regular rounds.

Littlejohn adaptors were mostly used on Daimler Armoured Cars and Tetrarch light tanks.

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/pol/wz_34]
The Polish army bought more than 135 chassis of the Citroen-Kegresse В2 10 CV halftrack armored cars, equipped with the Kegresse Р4Т track unit, from France in 1924. It was decided to make about 90 vehicles on their basis. The armored body was designed and manufactured in Poland, and its shape was very much alike that of French halftrack vehicles. Two prototypes had been made by 1925 and were designated Wzor 28 (Wz.28) - 1928 Pattern. The first mass-produced vehicles were ready in 1927. All 90 Wz.28 vehicles were manufactured between 1927 and 1930. When the vehicles were commissioned, it turned out that they failed to accomplish the assigned missions. Maximal speed was as low as 30 km/h, and cross-country capabilities were even worse. The vehicles required frequent maintenance, and the wear resistance of rubber tracks was very low. That is why the decision was made in 1933 to transform the vehicles from halftracks to wheeled cars. 
The first transformed vehicles saw tests from April to July 1934. It turned out that they behaved a bit worse than the Wz.28 on rough terrain, but much better on highway. The final decision to transform all Wz.28 vehicles and designate them Wzor 34 (Wz.34) – 1934 Pattern was made in July 1934. All Wz.28 vehicles were adjusted to Wz.34 standards before 1938. 
During the mobilization of 1939, 10 special squadrons were established and attached to 10 new armor battalions, which were organic of the Cavalry Brigades. Each squadron incorporated seven Wz.34 armored vehicles.
Reconnaissance and protection of motor convoys were the main missions of the Wz.34, as the vehicle could not be used as tank support due to weak armor and armament. However, due to acute shortage of armored cars, the Wz.34 was used to contain the German offensive and to support counterattacks, which resulted in heavy losses among the vehicles. They took part in nearly all battles on the Polish territory from September 1 to 27, 1939. 

[EncDescription_data/units/armoredcar/prk/BA_64B]
BA-64B Armored Car M1943

The combat deployment of the BA-64 armored car exposed a number of drawbacks. These included poor engine cooling, limited driver observability, weak suspension, and a narrow wheelbase which combined with a high center of gravity could lead to overturning. Work began on upgrading the armored car, and a new prototype entered tests in late October of 1942. This new vehicle, the BA-64B, had a wider wheelbase, better suspension featuring four hydraulic buffers on the front axle, a more powerful and better-cooled engine, and side hatches for improved driver observability. Testing found the new model to be a success, and mass production of the newer BA-64B was scheduled to begin at the GAZ plant on May 25. However, due to extensive damage caused by enemy bombings, the first cars were completed only in August of 1943. A total of 405 BA-64B and 1019 BA-64 cars were produced through the end of 1943.
As an improved variant of the BA-64, the BA-64B, was easy to produce and inexpensive. Its simple design featured bulletproof armor and a single DT machine gun which could also be used against airborne targets. This armored car served mainly as a reconnaissance, radio, and column support vehicle, and was a part of numerous recon, command, and radio military units, typically joined by tank and motorized infantry. A total of 5160 BA-64B cars were produced at the GAZ plant until the line was shut down in February of 1946. Most of these vehicles were lost in combat, but some BA-64Bs were in Soviet Army service through the early 1950s. A small number were exported, in particular to North Korea. The North Korean forces actively used the BA-64B in the Korean War, and at least one of these vehicles was captured as a trophy by the US forces.